<h1>Execution Time Middleware</h1>

<p>
    Execution Time Middleware is used to measure how much time a request takes to complete.
    It helps developers analyze application performance and identify slow requests.
    This middleware starts a timer before the request goes to the next middleware or controller,
    and stops the timer once the response comes back.
</p>

<hr />

<h3>Step 1: Create ExecutionTimeMiddleware.cs File</h3>

<p>
    Create a new file named <strong>ExecutionTimeMiddleware.cs</strong> inside the
    <strong>Middleware</strong> folder.
</p>

<pre>
<code>
using System.Diagnostics;

namespace Dotnet_Tutorials.Middlewares
{
    public class ExecutionTimeMiddleware
    {
        private readonly RequestDelegate _next;

        public ExecutionTimeMiddleware(RequestDelegate next)
        {
            _next = next;
        }

        public async Task InvokeAsync(HttpContext context)
        {
            var stopwatch = Stopwatch.StartNew();

            await _next(context);

            stopwatch.Stop();

            Console.WriteLine($"Request Time: {stopwatch.ElapsedMilliseconds} ms");
        }
    }
}
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h3>Step 2: Register the Middleware</h3>

<p>
    Register the middleware inside <strong>Program.cs</strong>.
</p>

<pre>
<code>
app.UseMiddleware&lt;ExecutionTimeMiddleware&gt;();
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Add this line before:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
app.UseRouting();
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h3>How It Works</h3>

<ul>
    <li>Middleware starts a stopwatch.</li>
    <li>Request moves to next middleware/controller using <strong>await _next(context)</strong>.</li>
    <li>After response returns, stopwatch stops.</li>
    <li>Total execution time is printed in console.</li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h3>Output Example</h3>

<pre>
<code>
Request Time: 245 ms
</code>
</pre>